// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// shealth-ohif (Shealth AI DICOM Viewer) — PRODUCTION CI/CD  [SWARM SERVICE]
//
// Repo:   https://github.com/ShealthAI/Shelath-Dicom-viewer.git
// Branch: shealth-main        ← the fork. `master` is upstream OHIF.
// Runs on: VPS-4 Jenkins (yt-phy-59-shelhealth, 103.216.93.59, web on lo:8080).
//
// THIS IS NOT test.jenkins WITH THE NAMES CHANGED. The deploy mechanism is
// different in three ways, and each one is a way to take prod down:
//
//   1. `ohif_app` is a SWARM SERVICE, not a `docker run` container. There is no
//      stop/rename/run and no rename-back. Deploy = `docker service update`,
//      rollback = `docker service rollback` (Swarm keeps the previous spec).
//      VPS-4 is a swarm MANAGER (VPS-3 is Leader) — the update is issued here.
//   2. The registry is VPS-3's `10.0.0.3:5000`, reached over WireGuard (wg0,
//      VPS-4 = 10.0.0.4), TLS with a private CA. NOT 127.0.0.1:5000 — that is
//      the VPS-7 test registry and does not exist on this host. The push also
//      needs `--with-registry-auth` on the service update, or any node that is
//      not VPS-3 cannot pull the new image and the task wedges in Pending.
//   3. The live service still carries the STOCK layout: image `ohif/app:latest`
//      and a read-only bind mount `/opt/volumes/ohif/app-config.js` →
//      `/usr/share/nginx/html/app-config.js`. That mount MUST be removed in the
//      same update that swaps the image. Our image bakes the config in; the
//      entrypoint's `gzip app-config.js` cannot unlink a bind-mounted source
//      (EBUSY) and, with `gzip_static always`, leaves a stale or 0-byte .gz
//      being served — a blank viewer, no error, exit code 0.
//      This pipeline READS the mount target out of the live service instead of
//      hardcoding it: `--mount-rm` with a wrong string is a silent no-op.
//
// PORT. Stock `ohif/app` is nginx-unprivileged listening on 8080, and prod Caddy
// dials `ohif_app:8080` over mesh-net. Our image's PORT is a build ARG that
// becomes an ENV and drives both the nginx template and the HEALTHCHECK, so the
// prod image is built with `--build-arg PORT=8080`. Build it with the default 80
// and the service comes up "healthy" on a port nobody dials → 502 on
// ohif.shealth.ai. Keep TARGET_PORT and the built image in agreement.
//
// PREREQ (one-time, on the VPS-4 HOST — not in the Jenkins container):
//   /etc/docker/certs.d/10.0.0.3:5000/ca.crt present, or the push fails with
//     400 Bad Request (plain HTTP against a TLS listener), NOT 401.
//   Managed file `registry-creds` (REG_USER/REG_PASS) matching VPS-3's htpasswd.
//   Preflight verifies both with one `docker login`, before the ~15 min build.
//   It does NOT test for the cert file: the push is performed by the docker
//   DAEMON, on the host, and this pipeline runs in a container with only
//   docker.sock. A file test here reads the wrong filesystem — it FATALs on a
//   correctly configured host and "passes" on a broken one. The login is the
//   only check the container boundary cannot fool.
//
// Jenkins config files: registry-creds.  Optional credential: teams-alert.
//
// checkout → preflight → [DEPLOY only: swarm + spec snapshot] → registry login →
// build → smoke the BAKED config → push → manual approval → rolling service
// update → verify through Caddy → rollback-on-fail.
//
// PREFLIGHT IS THREE STAGES, and the split is deliberate. Only the swarm one is
// gated on DEPLOY. With the cluster degraded (VPS-4 LocalNodeState=error, build
// 2 on 2026-08-21) an ungated swarm check kills the build before it compiles
// anything, so DEPLOY=false could not do what its description promises. The
// Dockerfile test and the registry login stay ungated: every build pushes.
//
// TWO THINGS SHARED WITH test.jenkins THAT ARE EASY TO GET WRONG
// ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1. The build file is `Dockerfile.shealth`, NOT `Dockerfile`. The plain
//    `Dockerfile` is upstream OHIF's and produces a stock viewer with none of the
//    GPU-tier rendering, WebGL context-loss recovery or branding. A build that
//    silently uses it looks successful and ships the wrong app.
// 2. app-config is BAKED IN by design. `app-config.js` is 0 bytes and the real
//    config lives in `app-config.js.gz`, served via `gzip_static always`. Setting
//    the APP_CONFIG env var, or bind-mounting app-config.js, replaces it. The
//    served byte count is the only signal — that is what Smoke and Verify check,
//    plus a grep for `_backend`, which stock OHIF configs do not contain.
//
// Tags are the build number only — never `latest`. The running image must be
// identifiable when someone asks what is in production.
//
// EDITING THE sh''' BLOCKS
// ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Groovy processes backslash escapes inside ''' ''' as well as inside " ". A `\\`
// in the source reaches the shell as one `\`, and `\n` reaches it as a real
// newline. Both have broken the test file before. Rules: never write a backslash
// to mean a literal backslash, never use `\n` in a printf/curl format — end with
// `; echo` instead. Trailing `\` line-continuations are fine.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

pipeline {
  agent any

  options {
    timestamps()
    disableConcurrentBuilds()
    buildDiscarder(logRotator(numToKeepStr: '30'))
    timeout(time: 90, unit: 'MINUTES')   // cold pnpm install + viewer build ~15 min, + approval wait
  }

  parameters {
    booleanParam(name: 'DEPLOY', defaultValue: true,
                 description: 'Roll ohif_app after a successful push. Off = build + smoke + push only (safe on any branch).')
    booleanParam(name: 'REQUIRE_APPROVAL', defaultValue: true,
                 description: 'Ask for a human OK before touching production. Turn off only for an automated window.')
    string(name: 'TARGET_PORT', defaultValue: '8080',
           description: 'Container port prod Caddy dials on mesh-net (ohif_app:PORT). Baked into the image as ARG/ENV PORT. Stock ohif/app = 8080. Changing this without changing the Caddyfile = 502.')
    string(name: 'MIN_CONFIG_BYTES', defaultValue: '10000',
           description: 'Fail if the served app-config.js is smaller than this. v6 = 12547 B, v20 = 14221 B. Near-zero means the baked config was clobbered.')
    string(name: 'APP_CONFIG', defaultValue: 'config/shealth.js',
           description: 'Build-time config compiled into the image. Leave alone. Only change for a one-off diagnostic build (e.g. config/default.js) — and then do not deploy it.')
    booleanParam(name: 'PRUNE', defaultValue: true,
                 description: 'Delete local shealth-ohif images older than the last 3 builds on this host. Registry tags are never pruned.')
  }

  // NO pollSCM and NO SCM trigger on this job. Production does not deploy because
  // someone pushed. Two Jenkins (VPS-4 and VPS-7) also watch this same repo and
  // would race. Start prod builds by hand, or from a release job.

  environment {
    IMAGE       = 'shealth-ohif'
    REGISTRY    = '10.0.0.3:5000'      // VPS-3 private registry over wg0. NOT 127.0.0.1:5000.
    TAG         = "${BUILD_NUMBER}"
    DOCKERFILE  = 'Dockerfile.shealth' // NOT Dockerfile — see header
    SERVICE     = 'ohif_app'           // stack `ohif`, service key `app`
    MESH        = 'mesh-net'           // attachable overlay shared with prod caddy
    CADDY       = 'caddy'              // standalone container that owns :80/:443 on VPS-4
    SMOKE_PORT  = '3010'               // free on VPS-4 per doc/ports.md
    SMOKE_NAME  = 'ohif_smoke'
    PUBLIC_HOST = 'ohif.shealth.ai'
  }

  stages {

    stage('Checkout') {
      steps {
        cleanWs()
        script { env.STEP = 'Checkout' }
        checkout scm
        script {
          env.GIT_SHA    = sh(script: 'git rev-parse --short HEAD',        returnStdout: true).trim()
          env.GIT_BRANCH_NAME = sh(script: 'git rev-parse --abbrev-ref HEAD || echo detached', returnStdout: true).trim()
          echo "Building ${IMAGE}:${TAG} @ ${env.GIT_SHA} (${env.GIT_BRANCH_NAME}) for PRODUCTION"
        }
      }
    }

    stage('Preflight') {
      steps {
        script { env.STEP = 'Preflight' }

        // Everything that can fail must fail here, with the fix in hand — not
        // 15 minutes later at the push, and certainly not half way through a
        // rolling update on production.
        sh '''
          set -e
          test -f "${DOCKERFILE}" || { echo "FATAL: ${DOCKERFILE} missing — wrong branch? (the fork branch is shealth-main)"; exit 1; }

          # NO CA FILE CHECK. /etc/docker/certs.d is read by the docker DAEMON on
          # the host; this runs inside the Jenkins container. See the header.
        '''
      }
    }

    // Swarm / service / network state only matters to a build that will DEPLOY.
    // Gated so DEPLOY=false does what its description promises — build + smoke
    // + push, runnable while the cluster is degraded. Build 2 (2026-08-21) died
    // here on LocalNodeState=error having built nothing, with DEPLOY irrelevant.
    stage('Preflight: swarm') {
      when { expression { return params.DEPLOY } }
      steps {
        script { env.STEP = 'Preflight: swarm' }
        sh '''
          set -e

          # A service update can only be issued from a manager. VPS-4 is one
          # (the `ubuntu` node is Leader). If Raft is degraded this is where we stop.
          #
          # DO NOT gate on {{.Swarm.LocalNodeState}}. That field latches on error
          # and does NOT clear when the swarm node recovers. On 2026-08-19 a cert
          # rotation restarted VPS-4's swarm node; it came back — Reachable to the
          # Leader, raft fine, `docker node ls` / `service ls` / `service ps` all
          # answering — but the flag stayed `error` and killed build 2 before it
          # compiled anything. Nothing logged `cluster exited`; nothing had exited.
          # Gate on what the deploy actually needs instead: a manager that answers.
          mgr=$(docker info --format '{{.Swarm.ControlAvailable}}')
          [ "$mgr" = "true" ] || { echo "FATAL: this node is not a swarm MANAGER. Run the job on VPS-4."; exit 1; }
          docker node ls >/dev/null 2>&1 || { echo "FATAL: manager unreachable / no quorum. 2 managers (this host + the Leader) — check both. DEPLOY=false still builds and pushes."; exit 1; }

          # Reported for the record only — never fatal. See above.
          state=$(docker info --format '{{.Swarm.LocalNodeState}}')
          [ "$state" = "active" ] || echo "WARNING: LocalNodeState=$state, but the control plane answers — continuing. Stale flag; clears only on a dockerd restart, which stops every container on this host (live-restore is off)."

          docker service inspect "${SERVICE}" >/dev/null 2>&1 \
            || { echo "FATAL: service ${SERVICE} does not exist. This job updates an existing service; it does not create one."; exit 1; }

          docker network inspect "${MESH}" >/dev/null \
            || { echo "FATAL: ${MESH} not present on this host."; exit 1; }
        '''

        // Snapshot the live spec BEFORE anything changes. `docker service rollback`
        // is the real rollback path, but a spec on disk is what you read at 3am
        // when rollback itself misbehaves.
        sh '''
          set -e
          docker service inspect "${SERVICE}" > "${SERVICE}.pre-${TAG}.json"
          echo "--- current spec ---"
          docker service inspect "${SERVICE}" --format 'image={{.Spec.TaskTemplate.ContainerSpec.Image}}  replicas={{if .Spec.Mode.Replicated}}{{.Spec.Mode.Replicated.Replicas}}{{else}}global{{end}}'
          docker service inspect "${SERVICE}" --format 'mounts={{json .Spec.TaskTemplate.ContainerSpec.Mounts}}'
          docker service inspect "${SERVICE}" --format 'ports={{json .Spec.EndpointSpec.Ports}}'
          docker service ps "${SERVICE}" --no-trunc --format '{{.Name}}  {{.Node}}  {{.CurrentState}}  {{.Image}}' | head -10
        '''

        script {
          // Read the app-config bind mount target out of the LIVE service. A
          // hardcoded --mount-rm string that does not match is accepted by the
          // CLI and silently removes nothing — the deploy then "succeeds" with
          // the stock config mounted over our baked one, i.e. a blank viewer.
          env.MOUNT_TARGET = sh(returnStdout: true, script: '''
            docker service inspect "${SERVICE}" --format '{{json .Spec.TaskTemplate.ContainerSpec.Mounts}}' \
              | grep -o '"Target":"[^"]*app-config[^"]*"' \
              | head -1 | sed 's/.*"Target":"//; s/"$//'
          ''').trim()

          env.CUR_IMAGE = sh(returnStdout: true, script:
            'docker service inspect "${SERVICE}" --format "{{.Spec.TaskTemplate.ContainerSpec.Image}}"').trim()

          env.REPLICAS = sh(returnStdout: true, script:
            'docker service inspect "${SERVICE}" --format "{{if .Spec.Mode.Replicated}}{{.Spec.Mode.Replicated.Replicas}}{{else}}global{{end}}"').trim()

          // Host-mode published ports cannot be handed from a task to its own
          // replacement: swarm stops the old task, the new one cannot bind, and
          // the service wedges Pending with prod down (the orthanc_pg lesson,
          // doc/work_2026-08-21.md). ohif_app has no published ports today; this
          // guard exists so a future change does not learn that the hard way.
          env.HOST_MODE_PORTS = sh(returnStdout: true, script: '''
            docker service inspect "${SERVICE}" --format '{{json .Spec.EndpointSpec.Ports}}' \
              | grep -c '"PublishMode":"host"' || true
          ''').trim()

          echo "current image : ${env.CUR_IMAGE}"
          echo "replicas      : ${env.REPLICAS}"
          echo "config mount  : ${env.MOUNT_TARGET ?: '(none — already removed, nothing to --mount-rm)'}"

          if (env.REPLICAS == '0') {
            echo "WARNING: ${env.SERVICE} is at 0 replicas. The update will apply to a service that runs nothing."
          }
          if (env.HOST_MODE_PORTS != '0' && env.HOST_MODE_PORTS != '') {
            error("FATAL: ${env.SERVICE} publishes ports in HOST mode. start-first would deadlock on the port bind. " +
                  "Such a change needs `docker service rm` + `docker service create` in a window, not this pipeline.")
          }
        }
        archiveArtifacts artifacts: "${SERVICE}.pre-${TAG}.json", allowEmptyArchive: true
      }
    }

    // Ungated: the push happens on every build, DEPLOY or not, so the login has
    // to be proved on every build — and still BEFORE the ~15 min build, not after.
    stage('Preflight: registry login') {
      steps {
        script { env.STEP = 'Preflight: registry login' }
        // Authenticate NOW, not after the build. A stale managed file or an
        // untrusted CA is a live failure mode and currently only surfaces at push.
        configFileProvider([configFile(fileId: 'registry-creds', variable: 'REG_FILE')]) {
          sh '''
            set +x
            . "$REG_FILE"
            if out=$(echo "$REG_PASS" | docker login ${REGISTRY} -u "$REG_USER" --password-stdin 2>&1); then
              echo "registry login OK (${REGISTRY})"
            else
              echo "$out"
              case "$out" in
                *400*)
                  echo "FATAL: 400 = plain HTTP sent to a TLS listener. The DAEMON does not trust"
                  echo "the VPS-3 registry CA. On the VPS-4 HOST, as root:"
                  echo "  mkdir -p /etc/docker/certs.d/10.0.0.3:5000"
                  echo "  cp <registry CA> /etc/docker/certs.d/10.0.0.3:5000/ca.crt"
                  echo "No daemon restart needed — certs.d is read per request."
                  ;;
                *401*|*authentication*|*unauthorized*)
                  echo "FATAL: 401 = CA trust is fine, the credentials are wrong."
                  echo "Update the managed file: Jenkins -> Manage Jenkins -> Managed files -> registry-creds"
                  ;;
                *)
                  echo "FATAL: registry ${REGISTRY} not reachable from this host."
                  echo "It lives on VPS-3 over WireGuard. Check:  ping -c1 10.0.0.3  and  wg show"
                  ;;
              esac
              exit 1
            fi
          '''
        }
      }
    }

    stage('Build image') {
      steps {
        script { env.STEP = 'Build image' }
        // BuildKit is REQUIRED: Dockerfile.shealth declares
        // `# syntax=docker/dockerfile:1.7-labs` and uses COPY --parents/--exclude.
        // Never set DOCKER_BUILDKIT=0 in this job.
        //
        // PORT is baked so the image listens where prod Caddy dials and the
        // image HEALTHCHECK probes the same port. See the header.
        sh '''
          set -e
          DOCKER_BUILDKIT=1 docker build \
            -f "${DOCKERFILE}" \
            --build-arg APP_CONFIG="${APP_CONFIG}" \
            --build-arg PORT="${TARGET_PORT}" \
            --build-arg BUILD_VERSION="${TAG}" \
            --build-arg BUILD_REVISION="${GIT_SHA}" \
            -t "${IMAGE}:${TAG}" .

          docker image inspect "${IMAGE}:${TAG}" \
            --format 'built {{.Id}}  version={{index .Config.Labels "org.opencontainers.image.version"}}  rev={{index .Config.Labels "org.opencontainers.image.revision"}}  exposed={{json .Config.ExposedPorts}}'

          # The image must have been built from OUR Dockerfile, not upstream's.
          docker image inspect "${IMAGE}:${TAG}" --format '{{index .Config.Labels "org.opencontainers.image.vendor"}}' \
            | grep -q "Shealth AI" || { echo "FATAL: vendor label missing — this is not the Shealth image (wrong Dockerfile?)"; exit 1; }
        '''
      }
    }

    stage('Smoke test') {
      // Exercise the image in isolation BEFORE it reaches the registry or prod.
      // This is where a clobbered app-config, a stock build, or a wrong PORT dies.
      steps {
        script { env.STEP = 'Smoke test' }
        sh '''
          set -e
          docker rm -f "${SMOKE_NAME}" 2>/dev/null || true
          # No -e APP_CONFIG and no -v app-config mount. Both replace the baked config.
          docker run -d --name "${SMOKE_NAME}" -p 127.0.0.1:${SMOKE_PORT}:${TARGET_PORT} "${IMAGE}:${TAG}"

          code=000
          for i in $(seq 1 30); do
            code=$(curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}' "http://127.0.0.1:${SMOKE_PORT}/" || true)
            [ "$code" = "200" ] && break
            sleep 1
          done
          [ "$code" = "200" ] || {
            echo "FAIL: / returned $code on port ${TARGET_PORT}"
            echo "If the container is up but nothing answers, the image was built with a different PORT than TARGET_PORT."
            docker logs --tail 40 "${SMOKE_NAME}"; exit 1; }

          # The entrypoint prints which path it took. "Not using custom APP_CONFIG"
          # is the correct line — anything else means the baked config was replaced.
          docker logs "${SMOKE_NAME}" 2>&1 | grep -E "APP_CONFIG|app-config" || true
          docker logs "${SMOKE_NAME}" 2>&1 | grep -q "Not using custom APP_CONFIG" \
            || { echo "FAIL: entrypoint rewrote app-config.js from the APP_CONFIG env var."; exit 1; }

          bytes=$(curl -s "http://127.0.0.1:${SMOKE_PORT}/app-config.js" | wc -c)
          echo "app-config.js served: ${bytes} bytes (floor ${MIN_CONFIG_BYTES})"
          if [ "$bytes" -lt "${MIN_CONFIG_BYTES}" ]; then
            echo "FAIL: app-config.js is ${bytes} bytes — the baked config is gone."
            echo "Check for an app-config.js bind mount or an APP_CONFIG env var."
            exit 1
          fi

          # Byte count alone cannot tell our config from a stock one of similar
          # size. `_backend` is the query param our config reads to pick the
          # DICOMweb backend; no upstream config mentions it.
          curl -s "http://127.0.0.1:${SMOKE_PORT}/app-config.js" | grep -q "_backend" \
            || { echo "FAIL: served config has no _backend handling — this is a STOCK OHIF config."; exit 1; }

          # gzip_static always + gunzip on: the .gz IS the config. Prove it survived.
          docker exec "${SMOKE_NAME}" test -s /usr/share/nginx/html/app-config.js.gz \
            || { echo "FAIL: app-config.js.gz missing or empty in the image"; exit 1; }

          # The image HEALTHCHECK catches "nginx alive, serving a broken shell".
          hs=none
          for i in $(seq 1 30); do
            hs=$(docker inspect -f '{{.State.Health.Status}}' "${SMOKE_NAME}" 2>/dev/null || echo none)
            [ "$hs" = "healthy" ] && break
            [ "$hs" = "unhealthy" ] && { echo "FAIL: container went unhealthy"; docker logs --tail 40 "${SMOKE_NAME}"; exit 1; }
            sleep 2
          done
          [ "$hs" = "healthy" ] || { echo "FAIL: never reached healthy (last=${hs})"; exit 1; }
          echo "smoke OK (health=${hs}, port=${TARGET_PORT})"
        '''
      }
      post {
        always { sh 'docker rm -f "${SMOKE_NAME}" 2>/dev/null || true' }
      }
    }

    stage('Push to registry') {
      // Push BEFORE the service update. Swarm nodes pull from the registry — a
      // locally-loaded image would need --no-resolve-image and only works on the
      // node that happens to hold it.
      // Numbered tag only, no :latest.
      steps {
        script { env.STEP = 'Push to registry' }
        configFileProvider([configFile(fileId: 'registry-creds', variable: 'REG_FILE')]) {
          sh '''
            set +x
            . "$REG_FILE"
            echo "$REG_PASS" | docker login ${REGISTRY} -u "$REG_USER" --password-stdin
            set -x
            docker tag  "${IMAGE}:${TAG}" "${REGISTRY}/${IMAGE}:${TAG}"
            docker push "${REGISTRY}/${IMAGE}:${TAG}"
          '''
        }
      }
    }

    stage('Approval') {
      when { expression { return params.DEPLOY && params.REQUIRE_APPROVAL } }
      steps {
        script { env.STEP = 'Approval' }
        timeout(time: 30, unit: 'MINUTES') {
          input message: "Roll PRODUCTION ${env.SERVICE} to ${env.REGISTRY}/${env.IMAGE}:${env.TAG} (${env.GIT_SHA})?\n" +
                         "current: ${env.CUR_IMAGE}\n" +
                         "mount-rm: ${env.MOUNT_TARGET ?: '(none)'}",
                ok: 'Deploy'
        }
      }
    }

    stage('Deploy ohif_app') {
      when { expression { return params.DEPLOY } }
      steps {
        script { env.STEP = 'Deploy' }
        // --with-registry-auth must be in the SAME command as --image: it ships
        // this login's credentials to the nodes so they can pull from 10.0.0.3:5000.
        // Without it a task on any node lands in Pending with "No such image".
        //
        // start-first: bring the new task up before killing the old one. Safe
        // because ohif_app publishes no host-mode port (checked in Preflight).
        // Note these update-* flags PERSIST in the service spec after this run.
        //
        // --detach=false makes the CLI wait for convergence and exit non-zero on
        // failure; --update-failure-action rollback means swarm has already put
        // the old spec back by the time we get that exit code.
        //
        // --mount-rm is added only when Preflight actually found the mount.
        script {
          def mountRm = env.MOUNT_TARGET ? "--mount-rm '${env.MOUNT_TARGET}' " : ""
          if (!env.MOUNT_TARGET) {
            echo "No app-config bind mount on the live service — skipping --mount-rm (already clean)."
          }
          sh """
            set -e
            docker service update \
              --with-registry-auth \
              --image '${env.REGISTRY}/${env.IMAGE}:${env.TAG}' \
              ${mountRm}--update-order start-first \
              --update-parallelism 1 \
              --update-delay 10s \
              --update-monitor 30s \
              --update-failure-action rollback \
              --rollback-order start-first \
              --detach=false \
              '${env.SERVICE}'
          """
        }
        sh '''
          set -e
          echo "--- post-update spec ---"
          docker service inspect "${SERVICE}" --format 'image={{.Spec.TaskTemplate.ContainerSpec.Image}}'
          docker service inspect "${SERVICE}" --format 'mounts={{json .Spec.TaskTemplate.ContainerSpec.Mounts}}'
          docker service ps "${SERVICE}" --no-trunc --format '{{.Name}}  {{.Node}}  {{.CurrentState}}  {{.Error}}' | head -10

          # The update converging is not the same as the mount being gone. A
          # --mount-rm whose target string did not match is a silent no-op, and
          # the viewer then serves the OLD mounted config with no error anywhere.
          if docker service inspect "${SERVICE}" --format '{{json .Spec.TaskTemplate.ContainerSpec.Mounts}}' | grep -q "app-config"; then
            echo "FAIL: the app-config bind mount is STILL on the service spec after --mount-rm."
            echo "The target string did not match. Read the exact target from:"
            echo "  docker service inspect ${SERVICE} --format '{{json .Spec.TaskTemplate.ContainerSpec.Mounts}}'"
            exit 1
          fi
        '''
      }
    }

    stage('Verify') {
      when { expression { return params.DEPLOY } }
      steps {
        script { env.STEP = 'Verify' }
        sh '''
          set -e

          # 1. A running task, on the image we just pushed, with no failed sibling.
          want="${REGISTRY}/${IMAGE}:${TAG}"
          ok=0
          for i in $(seq 1 60); do
            running=$(docker service ps "${SERVICE}" --filter desired-state=running --format '{{.CurrentState}}' | grep -c '^Running' || true)
            [ "$running" -ge 1 ] && { ok=1; break; }
            sleep 2
          done
          docker service ps "${SERVICE}" --no-trunc --format '{{.Name}}  {{.Node}}  {{.CurrentState}}  {{.Image}}  {{.Error}}' | head -10
          [ "$ok" = "1" ] || { echo "FAIL: no Running task for ${SERVICE}"; exit 1; }

          docker service ps "${SERVICE}" --filter desired-state=running --no-trunc --format '{{.Image}}' \
            | grep -q "${IMAGE}:${TAG}" \
            || { echo "FAIL: the running task is not on ${want} — swarm may have rolled back already."; exit 1; }

          # 2. Through the exact path prod Caddy uses: the service name on
          #    mesh-net, on TARGET_PORT. NOT the alias `app` — ohif_app and the old
          #    orthanc_app both claim it, so `app` can resolve to the wrong service.
          code=000
          for i in $(seq 1 30); do
            code=$(docker exec "${CADDY}" wget -S -O /dev/null "http://${SERVICE}:${TARGET_PORT}/" 2>&1 | grep -o 'HTTP/1.[01] [0-9]*' | tail -1 | grep -o '[0-9]*$' || true)
            [ "$code" = "200" ] && break
            sleep 2
          done
          echo "caddy -> ${SERVICE}:${TARGET_PORT}/ = ${code}"
          [ "$code" = "200" ] || {
            echo "FAIL: Caddy cannot reach ${SERVICE}:${TARGET_PORT}. This is the 502 gate."
            echo "Check: is ${CADDY} attached to ${MESH}?  docker inspect ${CADDY} --format '{{json .NetworkSettings.Networks}}'"
            exit 1; }

          # 3. The served config, over the wire, in production.
          bytes=$(docker exec "${CADDY}" wget -q -O - "http://${SERVICE}:${TARGET_PORT}/app-config.js" | wc -c)
          echo "prod app-config.js: ${bytes} bytes (floor ${MIN_CONFIG_BYTES})"
          [ "$bytes" -ge "${MIN_CONFIG_BYTES}" ] || { echo "FAIL: config ${bytes} B — baked config clobbered in prod."; exit 1; }
          docker exec "${CADDY}" wget -q -O - "http://${SERVICE}:${TARGET_PORT}/app-config.js" | grep -q "_backend" \
            || { echo "FAIL: prod is serving a STOCK OHIF config."; exit 1; }

          # 4. Front door, best-effort. Jenkins runs with network_mode: host on
          #    VPS-4, so 127.0.0.1:443 is Caddy. Non-fatal: DNS/TLS problems here
          #    are not this deploy's doing and must not trigger a rollback.
          curl -skI "https://${PUBLIC_HOST}/" --resolve "${PUBLIC_HOST}:443:127.0.0.1" | head -3 || true
        '''
      }
    }

    stage('Prune') {
      when { expression { return params.PRUNE } }
      steps {
        script { env.STEP = 'Prune' }
        // Local images on THIS host only. Registry tags are kept — they are the
        // rollback material for every other node.
        sh '''
          for repo in "${IMAGE}" "${REGISTRY}/${IMAGE}"; do
            docker images "$repo" --format '{{.Tag}}' \
              | grep -E '^[0-9]+$' | sort -rn | tail -n +4 \
              | while read t; do echo "Prune $repo:$t"; docker rmi "$repo:$t" 2>/dev/null || true; done
          done
        '''
      }
    }
  }

  post {
    success {
      script { notifyTeams('SUCCESS', "PROD ohif_app on ${env.IMAGE}:${env.TAG} — healthy through Caddy") }
    }
    failure {
      script {
        if (env.STEP == 'Deploy' || env.STEP == 'Verify') {
          echo "Failure @ ${env.STEP} — rolling ${env.SERVICE} back to the previous spec"
          // `docker service rollback` restores the WHOLE previous spec: the old
          // image AND the app-config bind mount that went with it. That is the
          // correct pairing — the stock image needs that mount.
          sh '''
            set +e
            docker service rollback --detach=false "${SERVICE}"
            docker service ps "${SERVICE}" --no-trunc --format '{{.Name}}  {{.Node}}  {{.CurrentState}}  {{.Image}}' | head -10
            docker exec "${CADDY}" wget -S -O /dev/null "http://${SERVICE}:${TARGET_PORT}/" 2>&1 | head -1
            echo "If the service is still wedged, the spec before this build is archived as ${SERVICE}.pre-${TAG}.json"
          '''
          notifyTeams('FAILED', "Failed @ ${env.STEP} — ohif_app ROLLED BACK to ${env.CUR_IMAGE}")
        } else {
          notifyTeams('FAILED', "Failed @ ${env.STEP} — production untouched")
        }
      }
    }
    always {
      sh 'docker rm -f "${SMOKE_NAME}" 2>/dev/null || true'
      sh 'docker logout ${REGISTRY} 2>/dev/null || true'
    }
  }
}

def notifyTeams(String status, String detail) {
  try {
    withCredentials([string(credentialsId: 'teams-alert', variable: 'TEAMS_WEBHOOK')]) {
      def color = (status == 'SUCCESS') ? '2EB67D' : 'E01E5A'
      def payload = """{
        "@type":"MessageCard","@context":"http://schema.org/extensions",
        "themeColor":"${color}","summary":"shealth-ohif PROD ${status}",
        "sections":[{
          "activityTitle":"shealth-ohif PROD build #${env.BUILD_NUMBER} — ${status}",
          "facts":[
            {"name":"Status","value":"${status}"},
            {"name":"Detail","value":"${detail}"},
            {"name":"Commit","value":"${env.GIT_SHA ?: 'n/a'}"},
            {"name":"Image","value":"${env.REGISTRY}/${env.IMAGE}:${env.TAG}"},
            {"name":"Service","value":"${env.SERVICE}"}
          ],"markdown":true}]}"""
      writeFile file: 'teams.json', text: payload
      sh 'curl -s -H "Content-Type: application/json" -d @teams.json "$TEAMS_WEBHOOK" || true'
      sh 'rm -f teams.json || true'
    }
  } catch (e) {
    echo "Teams notify skipped: ${e.message}"
  }
}
